Fine-tuning on collision-free demonstrations produces genuine collision avoidance: LoRA-adapted Pi0.5 raises CAR by ${\sim}$28 percentage points with zero frozen episodes. Does SDF geometric supervision contribute additional avoidance beyond what demonstrations provide? We compare LoRA-only, LoRA+SDF ($\lambda = 0.005$), and LoRA+SDF ($\lambda = 0.01$) on Pi0.5, all evaluated at the 30K-step checkpoint.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Avoidance source attribution on SafeLIBERO. SDF at $\lambda \leq 0.01$ adds no measurable CAR beyond LoRA-only ($p > 0.1$); $\lambda = 0.1$ shows a marginal signal ($p \approx 0.071$, two-tailed permutation test, $n{=}2$) that requires additional seeds. All fine-tuned models exhibit 0\% frozen rate and displacement $>$\,0.37\,m.}
\label{tab:avoidance_source}
\vspace{2pt}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Method & Seeds & TSR (\%) & CAR (\%) & EE Disp (m) \\
\midrule
Pi0.5 baseline & 3 & 74.7\,$\pm$\,1.5 & 26.8\,$\pm$\,1.6 & 0.367 \\
\midrule
LoRA-only (no SDF) & 3 & 51.5\,$\pm$\,0.0 & 54.83\,$\pm$\,2.25 & 0.391 \\
LoRA+SDF $\lambda{=}0.005$ & 3 & 54.0\,$\pm$\,3.0 & 54.5\,$\pm$\,2.29 & 0.387 \\
LoRA+SDF $\lambda{=}0.01$ & 3 & 53.8\,$\pm$\,1.5 & 56.33\,$\pm$\,1.61 & 0.389 \\
LoRA+SDF $\lambda{=}0.1$\textsuperscript{$\dagger$} & 2 & 48.0\,$\pm$\,1.4 & 58.25\,$\pm$\,0.35 & 0.394 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\vspace{1pt}
{\footnotesize \textsuperscript{$\dagger$}30K checkpoint, two of three seeds; third seed in progress.}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:avoidance_source} shows that collision-free demonstrations alone raise CAR by ${\sim}$28 percentage points (26.8\% $\to$ 54.83\%) with zero frozen episodes and displacement above 0.37\,m, which confirms genuine avoidance. Adding SDF supervision produces no measurable benefit: LoRA-only CAR (54.83\%) is within 0.33\,pp of $\lambda = 0.005$ and statistically indistinguishable from $\lambda = 0.01$ (56.33\%, $p > 0.1$; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_null}). A Phase~2 ablation ($\lambda = 0$ after step 5K) yields CAR\,=\,57.5\%\footnote{Single seed (s2); computational constraints precluded additional seeds for this ablation.}; this provides preliminary evidence that SDF gradients produce no lasting behavioral effect. The per-task CAR ordering is preserved across all conditions (chocolate\_pudding highest, bbq\_sauce lowest; Figure~\ref{fig:pertask_fingerprint}) and invariant to $\lambda$ across a 20$\times$ range; avoidance patterns reflect the demonstration distribution rather than geometric supervision.

\paragraph{CAR--TSR trade-off.}\label{sec:tsr_collapse}
Fine-tuning degrades TSR by ${\sim}$21--23\,pp (74.7\% $\to$ 51.5--54.0\%), a consequence of distributional shift from the pre-trained prior rather than learned paralysis (all models retain $>$\,0.37\,m displacement and 0\% frozen episodes). The SSR/TSR ratio increases from 0.39 (baseline) to 0.51--0.57 (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_ssr}): among task-completing episodes, a larger fraction avoids collisions after fine-tuning.

\paragraph{Dose-response at higher loss weights.}\label{sec:lambda_high}
Increasing $\lambda$ by 20$\times$ to 0.1 yields CAR\,=\,58.25\%\,$\pm$\,0.35 at the 30K checkpoint (two of three seeds), above the null distribution (mean 54.67\%, $\sigma = 2.04$; two-tailed permutation $p \approx 0.071$)\footnote{One-tailed permutation $p = 0.036$; we report the conservative two-tailed value in the main text. A directional prior is justified because the SDF loss is designed to \emph{reduce} collisions.}. This departure is suggestive but inconclusive at $n = 2$. A stepwise ablation across six checkpoints (16K--28K) shows no sustained trend (mean 55.3\%, within 0.3$\sigma$ of the null; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_stepwise}), and per-task CAR ordering at 30K is preserved across all other conditions (Figure~\ref{fig:lambda_car}). The response is thus dose-dependent: SDF is fully redundant at $\lambda \leq 0.01$ ($p > 0.1$), with a marginal signal at $\lambda = 0.1$ whose modest effect size (${\sim}$3.6\,pp) and limited sample preclude strong conclusions. Even at elevated $\lambda$, avoidance patterns remain driven by demonstration distribution rather than geometric supervision.

