{"success": true, "data": {"stdout": "\begin{abstract}
Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models report high Collision Avoidance Rates (CAR) on safety benchmarks, yet we show these metrics are contaminated by action collapse, where near-zero motor output produces zero collisions through inaction rather than avoidance.
On SafeLIBERO, an SDF-augmented Pi0 achieves 91.5\% CAR while 99.6\% of its safe episodes exhibit end-effector (EE) displacement below 0.1\,mm. The robot is frozen, not safe.
We introduce displacement-filtered CAR (dfCAR), which excludes sub-threshold episodes. The bimodal displacement distribution makes dfCAR robust across a $[2, 200]$\,mm threshold range.
We further show that end-effector-only Signed Distance Field (SDF) supervision fails to deliver collision avoidance. At inference time, ground-truth oracle refinement reduces CAR below unrefined baselines because the SDF gradient drives unmonitored arm links into obstacles. During training, collision-free demonstrations account for all measured avoidance gains at $\lambda \leq 0.01$ (${\sim}$28 percentage points). SDF supervision adds no significant benefit at $\lambda \leq 0.01$ ($p > 0.1$) and only a marginal signal at $\lambda = 0.1$ ($p \approx 0.071$, two-tailed permutation test, $n{=}2$).
Displacement filtering is necessary for meaningful VLA safety evaluation. EE-only geometric supervision cannot reliably be credited with collision avoidance, nor expected to deliver it.
\end{abstract}
